.TH SIN 3M  "August 1, 1992"
.UC 4
.de Pi		\" PI stuff sign
.if n \\
\\$2pi\\$1
.if t \\
\\$2\\(*p\\$1
..
.ds up \fIulp\fR
.SH NAME
sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2 \- trigonometric functions
and their inverses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double sin(double \fIx\fP);
.PP
.B double cos(double \fIx\fP);
.PP
.B double tan(double \fIx\fP);
.PP
.B double asin(double \fIx\fP);
.PP
.B double acos(double \fIx\fP);
.PP
.B double atan(double \fIx\fP);
.PP
.B double atan2(double \fIy\fP, double \fIx\fP);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sin, cos and tan
return trigonometric functions of radian arguments x.
.PP
\fIAsin\fR returns the arc sine in the range 
.Pi /2 \-
to
.Pi /2.
.PP
\fIAcos\fR returns the arc cosine in the range 0 to
.Pi.
.PP
\fIAtan\fR returns the arc tangent in the range
.Pi /2 \-
to
.Pi /2.
.PP
\fIAtan2\fR computes the principal value of the arc tangent of
\fBy/x\fR, using the signs of both arguments to determine the
quadrant of the return value.  The arc tangent returned is in 
the range [\-
.Pi 
, +
.Pi 
]
.SH ERROR (due to roundoff, etc.)
Let P stand for the number stored in the computer in place of
.Pi " = 3.14159 26535 89793 23846 26433 ... ."
Let \*(lqtrig\*(rq stand for one of
\*(lqsin\*(rq, \*(lqcos\*(rq or \*(lqtan\*(rq.
Then the expression \*(lqtrig(x)\*(rq
in a program actually produces an approximation to
.Pi /P), trig(x\(**
and \*(lqatrig(x)\*(rq approximates
.Pi )\(**atrig(x). (P/
The approximations are close.

In the codes that run on other machines, P differs from
.Pi
by a fraction of an \*(up; the difference matters only if the argument
x is huge, and even then the difference is likely to be swamped by
the uncertainty in x.  Besides, every trigonometric identity that
does not involve
.Pi
explicitly is satisfied equally well regardless of whether
.Pi . "P = "
For instance,
.if n \
sin(x)**2+cos(x)**2\0=\01
.if t \
sin\u\s62\s10\d(x)+cos\u\s62\s10\d(x)\0=\01
and sin(2x)\0=\02\|sin(x)cos(x) to within a few \*(ups no matter how big
x may be.  Therefore the difference between P and
.Pi
is most unlikely to affect scientific and engineering computations.
.SH SEE ALSO
math(3M), hypot(3M), sinh(3M), sqrt(3M)
